Fix out-of-bounds stack write in tre_parse when a wide hex escape
\x{...} contains more hex digits than the destination buffer holds.

A pattern with a long \x{...} escape, such as one carrying 48 hex digits
"\x{1F60BB...B0}", writes past a stack buffer:

    ==1==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow
    WRITE of size 1
        #0 tre_parse    src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:1516
        #1 tre_compile  src/extra/tre/tre-compile.c:1920
        #2 tre_regcompb src/extra/tre/regcomp.c:150
        #3 do_grep      src/main/grep.c:1367

The "Wide char" branch of the \x escape handler copies the hex digits
between the braces into the 32-byte stack buffer tmp with a loop that
increments the index i on every digit but never checks it against
sizeof(tmp).  Once the escape holds 32 or more hex digits the stores at
"tmp[i] = ..." run off the end of the buffer, and so does the trailing
"tmp[i] = 0".  (A 32-digit value overflows the long that strtol() then
produces anyway, so no legitimate pattern relies on that many digits.)

Reject an over-long escape with REG_EBRACE -- which is how a non-hex,
non-'}' character in the same escape is already handled one line below.

This patch originally also tightened the loop guard from
"ctx->re_end - ctx->re >= 0" to "ctx->re < ctx->re_end" (an unterminated
"\x{1" read one element past the pattern).  That half landed in trunk
as r90814 (PR#18995); the bound on the digit count did not.

Note this is an out-of-bounds *write*.  Reachable from R via grep(),
sub(), gsub(), regexpr() and friends (default perl=FALSE engine)
whenever the pattern comes from an untrusted source, e.g.
grep(paste0("\\x{", strrep("B", 40), "}"), "x").

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer.

Upstream status: NOT YET REPORTED to the R Bugzilla as of 2026-11-01;
held for the bundled-TRE update (r-devel/r-svn PR #302).
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

--- a/src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c
+++ b/src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c
@@ -1517,6 +1517,8 @@
 			    break;
 			  if (tre_isxdigit(ctx->re[0]))
 			    {
+			      if (i >= (int)sizeof(tmp) - 1)
+				return REG_EBRACE;
 			      tmp[i] = (char)ctx->re[0];
 			      i++;
 			      ctx->re++;
